# OpenAI Privacy Filter Add-on Implementation Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (if subagents available) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Add OpenAI Privacy Filter (`openai/privacy-filter`, the local `opf` CLI) as an optional eighth detection layer that augments — never replaces — the existing seven-layer redactor. Off by default. Runs only at condensation and export read paths, never on per-turn temp writes. Failure soft-falls back to the existing layers with an actionable warning.

**Architecture:** A new layer 8 (`detectOPF`) plugs into the existing `taggedRegion` merge machinery in `redact/redact.go`. Two new entry points (`StringWithPrivacyFilter` and `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter`) gate the OPF layer; the plain entry points never invoke it. OPF is reached through a `Runtime` interface in a new `redact/opf_runtime/` subpackage; v1 implementation shells out to the user-installed `opf` binary. Settings live as a third sibling alongside `PII` and `CustomRedactions` in `RedactionSettings`. The runtime decision (shell-out v1 vs daemon follow-up) is gated by a benchmark in Chunk 4; everything north of the `Runtime` interface is identical in both worlds.

**Tech Stack:** Go 1.26, standard library (`os/exec`, `encoding/json`, `context`, `log/slog`, `regexp`), the existing `redact` package layered detector, the existing `interactive` package for TTY detection, the existing `paths` and `settings` packages for config plumbing. Tests use `t.Parallel()` per `CLAUDE.md` and isolate filesystem state via `t.TempDir()` and `t.Chdir()`.

**Spec:** [`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-12-openai-privacy-filter-addon-design.md`](../specs/2026-05-12-openai-privacy-filter-addon-design.md)

**Skills to read before executing:**
- `@superpowers:test-driven-development` — every code change in this plan goes through Red → Green → Refactor.
- `@superpowers:verification-before-completion` — never claim a step is done without running and observing the verification command.

**Commit batching:**
Each chunk corresponds to one git commit at its boundary. Do not commit per task. Stage as you go inside the chunk; commit only at the explicit chunk-end commit step. This keeps PR review to four logical commits per the project's review preference.

